## 6. Authentication Flow

```
Flutter app
   │  supabase.auth.signUp() / signInWithPassword()   ← calls Supabase directly, OR
   │  POST /auth/register / /auth/login               ← calls your Express wrapper
   ▼
Supabase Auth issues { access_token, refresh_token }
   │
   ▼
Flutter stores tokens, attaches `Authorization: Bearer <access_token>`
to every subsequent request to your Express API
   │
   ▼
Express `requireAuth` middleware calls supabaseAdmin.auth.getUser(token)
   │
   ▼
req.user.id available to every controller/service
```

**Decision point:** you can let Flutter call Supabase Auth *directly* (simpler, one less hop) and only send the resulting access token to your Express API for everything else. Or you can proxy auth through Express (as in Step 2's prompts) if you want a single API surface for the mobile team. Either works; direct-to-Supabase is slightly less code to maintain.

On first login, create the matching `Profile` row (id = Supabase user id) if it doesn't exist yet — this is a one-time "provisioning" step you can put in the login controller or in a Supabase Auth webhook (Database Webhooks → `on auth.users insert`).

